<section class="course">

<h2>📖 How to Prepare, Study, and Write a Sermon</h2>

<p>
Click each lesson to learn the process step by step.
</p>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>1. Pray and Begin With the Right Purpose</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      Sermon preparation should begin with prayer and a desire to faithfully
      communicate God's Word.
    </p>

    <h4>Ask yourself:</h4>

    <ul>
      <li>What does Scripture say?</li>
      <li>What is the main truth I need to understand?</li>
      <li>What does this passage teach about God?</li>
      <li>What does this passage teach about people?</li>
      <li>How should this truth affect the listener?</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tip">
      <strong>Remember:</strong>
      Start with Scripture, not with the sermon idea you already want to prove.
    </div>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>2. Choose Your Sermon Topic or Passage</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      Choose either a biblical topic or a specific passage of Scripture.
    </p>

    <p>
      Examples include faith, forgiveness, salvation, prayer, obedience,
      temptation, love, hope, suffering, or a particular chapter of the Bible.
    </p>

    <h4>Write down:</h4>

    <ul>
      <li>Working sermon title</li>
      <li>Main topic</li>
      <li>Purpose of the sermon</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tip">
      Your title can change later. At this stage you are establishing
      the direction of your study.
    </div>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>3. Read the Passage Carefully</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      Read the passage several times before beginning to build your sermon.
    </p>

    <h4>Look for:</h4>

    <ul>
      <li>Repeated words</li>
      <li>Commands</li>
      <li>Promises</li>
      <li>Warnings</li>
      <li>Questions</li>
      <li>Contrasts</li>
      <li>People</li>
      <li>Events</li>
      <li>Causes and effects</li>
      <li>The main message of the passage</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="warning">
      Do not immediately jump to commentary or other people's explanations.
      First spend time observing the biblical text yourself.
    </div>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>4. Study the Context</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      A verse should be understood in the context of the surrounding passage.
    </p>

    <h4>Ask:</h4>

    <ul>
      <li>Who wrote the passage?</li>
      <li>Who was it written to?</li>
      <li>What was happening?</li>
      <li>What comes before this passage?</li>
      <li>What comes after it?</li>
      <li>What problem or question is being addressed?</li>
      <li>What is the author's main point?</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tip">
      Context protects you from taking a verse out of its intended meaning.
    </div>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>5. Find the Main Biblical Truth</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      After studying the passage, summarize what it is teaching in one clear
      sentence.
    </p>

    <h4>Example:</h4>

    <p>
      Instead of having five different ideas, identify the central biblical
      truth that holds the sermon together.
    </p>

    <div class="tip">
      If you cannot explain the main point in a simple sentence,
      you probably need to study the passage more.
    </div>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>6. Study Important Hebrew and Greek Words</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      Sometimes an important biblical word deserves deeper study.
    </p>

    <p>
      Use Hebrew and Greek resources to investigate the original word,
      its meaning, and how it is used.
    </p>

    <h4>Study:</h4>

    <ul>
      <li>Strong's number</li>
      <li>Original word</li>
      <li>Transliteration</li>
      <li>Basic meaning</li>
      <li>How the word is used in Scripture</li>
      <li>How the word functions in your passage</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="warning">
      Do not build an entire doctrine from one dictionary definition.
      The context of Scripture remains important.
    </div>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>7. Use a Bible Dictionary</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      A Bible dictionary can help explain people, places, objects,
      customs, subjects, and biblical concepts.
    </p>

    <p>
      Use the dictionary to understand the background of what you are studying,
      not to replace the Bible itself.
    </p>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>8. Use a Concordance and Cross-References</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      A concordance helps you find where important words and subjects appear
      elsewhere in Scripture.
    </p>

    <h4>Look for:</h4>

    <ul>
      <li>Related verses</li>
      <li>Parallel passages</li>
      <li>Repeated biblical themes</li>
      <li>Other passages that explain the subject</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tip">
      Let Scripture help explain Scripture.
    </div>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>9. Compare Bible Translations</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      Comparing translations can help you see wording differences and
      clarify difficult passages.
    </p>

    <p>
      BibleMastrey can provide multiple translations such as KJV, WEB,
      and ASV.
    </p>

    <div class="warning">
      Different wording does not automatically mean different meaning.
      Always return to the biblical context.
    </div>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>10. Read Bible Commentaries</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      Commentaries allow you to compare your observations with the work
      of experienced Bible teachers and commentators.
    </p>

    <h4>Use commentaries to:</h4>

    <ul>
      <li>Discover historical background</li>
      <li>Consider different interpretations</li>
      <li>Identify difficult passages</li>
      <li>Check your understanding</li>
      <li>Find additional observations</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tip">
      Commentary should help you study Scripture, not become a substitute
      for studying Scripture.
    </div>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>11. Develop the Main Sermon Point</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      Now turn your study into one clear sermon statement.
    </p>

    <p>
      The listener should be able to understand what the sermon is ultimately
      trying to communicate.
    </p>

    <h4>A strong main point should be:</h4>

    <ul>
      <li>Biblical</li>
      <li>Clear</li>
      <li>Simple</li>
      <li>Memorable</li>
      <li>Connected to the main passage</li>
    </ul>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>12. Build Your Supporting Points</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      Divide the main truth into a few supporting points.
    </p>

    <p>
      Three points are often useful, but you do not have to force every sermon
      into exactly three points.
    </p>

    <h4>Each point should answer:</h4>

    <ul>
      <li>What does Scripture say?</li>
      <li>What does it mean?</li>
      <li>Why does it matter?</li>
      <li>How should we respond?</li>
    </ul>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>13. Explain the Scripture</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      Do not simply quote a verse and move on.
      Explain what the passage means.
    </p>

    <p>
      The listener should understand how the Scripture supports the point
      you are making.
    </p>

    <div class="tip">
      Scripture should be the foundation of the sermon, not decoration added
      to a message that was already written.
    </div>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>14. Apply the Biblical Truth</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      Ask how the truth of Scripture should affect everyday life.
    </p>

    <h4>Application may involve:</h4>

    <ul>
      <li>Something to believe</li>
      <li>Something to change</li>
      <li>Something to stop</li>
      <li>Something to start</li>
      <li>Something to remember</li>
      <li>Someone to forgive</li>
      <li>Someone to serve</li>
      <li>A biblical command to obey</li>
    </ul>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>15. Write the Introduction</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      The introduction should help the listener understand why the sermon
      matters.
    </p>

    <h4>A simple structure:</h4>

    <ol>
      <li>Introduce the subject.</li>
      <li>Identify the question or problem.</li>
      <li>Introduce the Scripture.</li>
      <li>Lead into the main truth.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      Do not make the introduction longer than the sermon needs.
    </p>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>16. Write the Conclusion</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      The conclusion brings the sermon back to its main truth.
    </p>

    <h4>A good conclusion can:</h4>

    <ul>
      <li>Review the main point</li>
      <li>Summarize the supporting points</li>
      <li>Call for biblical application</li>
      <li>Leave the listener with a clear takeaway</li>
    </ul>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>17. Put the Sermon Together</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>A simple sermon structure might look like this:</p>

    <ol>
      <li>Title</li>
      <li>Introduction</li>
      <li>Main Scripture</li>
      <li>Context</li>
      <li>Main biblical truth</li>
      <li>Point 1</li>
      <li>Point 2</li>
      <li>Point 3</li>
      <li>Supporting Scriptures</li>
      <li>Explanation</li>
      <li>Application</li>
      <li>Conclusion</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="tip">
      The exact structure can change depending on the passage and purpose.
    </div>

  </div>
</div>


<div class="lesson">
  <div class="lesson-header">
    <h3>18. Review the Sermon for Biblical Accuracy</h3>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <h4>Before teaching, ask:</h4>

    <ul>
      <li>Is my main point actually supported by Scripture?</li>
      <li>Did I take any verses out of context?</li>
      <li>Did I explain the passage accurately?</li>
      <li>Did I separate my opinion from what Scripture actually says?</li>
      <li>Are my supporting verses relevant?</li>
      <li>Is the sermon understandable?</li>
      <li>Is the application biblical and practical?</li>
      <li>Does the conclusion return to the main truth?</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="warning">
      A good sermon is not simply one that sounds powerful.
      It must also faithfully communicate Scripture.
    </div>

  </div>
</div>

</section>


